## Findings (batch 1, author's words preserved)

EXP 2 PRESENTATION
1. Exp 2 must be presented as a 4-arm 2×2 protocol FROM THE START — text
   still says "The experiment runs two arms" somewhere despite 0567.
2. Annex B agents table: colwidth auto; drop Country column.
3. Qwen version is wrong in the text — author recalls 3.7 was used.
   Verify against artifacts (models.yaml / outputs), fix per ground truth.
4. The 2025-05-20 compose decision is noise — delete.
5. "Test before blasting policy" mention = noise — delete.
6. Phase A is underexplained. Rework: clear crossref to the base prompt
   (sans-serif, boxed); then the metaprompt verbatim (same formatting).
   The text MUST describe the protocol validation by the models
   themselves — how we iterated, e.g. accommodated their request that
   the harness not be one of the four tested. (Sources: the synopsys
   comment files in slides/manuscript/, experiments/ docs.)
7. Phase C must not be mentioned anywhere — it does not exist.
8. Doc-07 is noise for the reader — remove the codename.

ANNEX A (EXP 1 SPEC)
9. Delete the whole "Archived baseline prompt" subsubsection — useless
   coding archeology, we don't present its results. (Tests pin its
   quote: test_annex_baseline_prompt_carries_as_sent_status_vocabulary,
   verbatim-anchor tests, brief entry annex-quote-anchor — update.)
10. Format the (analysis-cohort) prompt in sans serif.
11. "Trailing newline strip by the worker" = too much detail — delete.
12. Drop the language-family considerations entirely — irrelevant, false
    (models are natively multilingual), and removes the ridiculous claim
    of multiple representatives for all three languages.

ANNEX C (RECOGNITION MATRIX) + §7.1
13. Drop "names read left to right, no rotation" — leftover generation
    instruction. Sweep for this cruft class (instructions surviving as
    prose).
14. Drop the parenthesis about "common cause, no producer–consumer
    chaining" — data-flow organization detail.
15. Annex C intro: shorten much more — it repeats the caption.
16. Table status-difficulty "recognition rate" definition is obscure.
    Rework: "the column shows the average probability of being found by
    an Exp 1 model run" AND move the composition table into §7.1 as the
    justification — or drop the table. Author prefers rework + move.
17. §7.1 (and many passages) tone overly complicated, not the author's.
    Author's intended message: "The task is hard because it is a
    long-tail distribution. As explained in the intro, it is easy to
    know the existing power plants, but there are many abandoned or
    recently proposed projects which have low visibility [composition
    table goes here]."

ANNEX E (SCREEN VALIDATION)
18. Consider dropping Annex E and its references — repeats with weak
    statistical power what Figure 5 shows better. (Brief
    rho-caveat-conclusion requires \ref{sec:annex-screen} next to ρ in
    the Conclusion; tests key on it — retarget to the figure, keep the
    in-sample caveat.)

FIGURE 5
19. Figure 5 lost the model colors — regression; fix plot script,
    regenerate.
20. Figure 5: Deepseek-v4-flash label should be to the left.

STRUCTURE / STYLE GLOBAL
21. §7 has NUMBERED subsection headings while all other sections respect
    the writing guide (no numbered subsections). Convert (mind
    \ref{sec:fusion} call sites and label-keyed tests).
22. Main text must not refer to code — only annexes may. Figure captions
    refer to annexes for details. Sweep.
23. The matching algorithm has a name (flow) — use it.
24. ADR-3 reference is noise like Doc-07 — sweep all internal codenames.
25. Sweep for promises (again): e.g. any "deferred to post-conference
    analysis" phrasing.
26. Sweep register: this is a technical report, not a lab journal.

§5 (EXP 1)
30. Cohort presentation: just say the models are listed on Figure 5 and
    include Claude, DeepSeek, Mistral and Qwen at various sizes from
    workstation to frontier.

INTRO + §4 + ANNEX F
27. Intro rework: do NOT enumerate three contributions + three results.
    Contribute THE BENCHMARK, which includes the four-dimension quality
    score as the computable metric. Drop two-grain and STANAG from the
    intro. "Reference-free criteria suffice to screen out weakest runs
    and models." Drop the "targeted pipeline design narrows the gap"
    charabia. Intro target length: one page.
28. §4 too long: drop the paragraph "Each integration... \ref annex F".
29. Annex F (capability rollout): also note the emerging capability to
    swarm, orchestrate, and act as a team.

## Findings (batch 2, author's words preserved)

§3 (QUALITY DIMENSIONS)
31. "Defining good as scoring zero on none of the twelve dimensions" is a
    double negative. Rephrase: define bad runs as those with at least one
    zero score in any dimension.
32. Float Figure 3 two pages earlier, on its own page (as Figure 4 is).
33. "solidly and firmly" in the same paragraph = AI English — delete.
34. The paper keeps referencing "a bar" that is never defined. Define
    clearly or drop.
35. "A seeded recall bar" paragraphs verbose and obscure — use plain term
    "answer key" instead.

TABLE 1 + FIGURE 6
36. Table 1 (Exp 2): repeats data visible in Figure 6 — move Table 1 to
    an annex.
37. Figure 6 is hard to read — split the two panels into two separate
    figures.
38. Sweep noise phrases: "now scored for all runs", "minimum attainable
    p…", "bear evidence", "we don't do statistical tests", "We defer more
    observations with more models" (the last is a promise — delete).

§7.2 (SCREENING WITHOUT REFERENCE)
39. §7.2 should refer to Figure 5, not Annex E (to be dropped). Message:
    scores without reference correlate with precision → we can detect bad
    models and screen them out. Then introduce the Admiralty grading:
    consider models as information sources, runs as information packages.
    Note that model quality scoring could be useful for fusion.

§8 (DISCUSSION)
40. "A consistent reading summarized in introduction" = how to NOT say a
    thing — drop the sentence. Opening: say this section examines limits
    and potential of the offered benchmark. Condense to 1.5 pages. Include
    a discussion of method quality scoring (time & money resources vs
    quality of results) vs table quality scoring (=run) vs model scoring
    (see docs/ discussion note).

§9 (FUTURE RESEARCH)
41. Condense to 1.5 pages. State: we will continue towards a method that
    scores all green on Table S1 — finds all the projects, only the
    projects, each justified by 2 primary sources, with correct attributes.
    That method requires research on 6 axes: information fusion, external
    coherence scoring, tracking both events and states, knowledge base
    architecture, replication on country and sectors. Reorder the axes;
    weave the literature review with the 6 axes instead of juxtaposing.
    (Author invites pushback on ordering.)

CONCLUSION / OVERARCHING CLAIMS
42. "and the evidence points toward a conjecture about why: the binding
    constraint is the data, not the model. Each claim in that sentence
    rests on a measured result." — sounds like meaningless vague
    generality. Drop. Replace with evidence: with data, Mistral large
    works as well as Opus → we may hope to solve this task without an
    arms race, via a sourcing pipeline ("On n'a pas de GPU mais on a des
    idées").

